There's a definite advantage to reaching Minetown early, and if one of
your goals is to lead off the game by getting cheap protection, it's
going to help a lot to avoid becoming, say, level 10.

In general, I only worry about accumulating enough gold for the first
shot of protection -- after that, I just pick up armor until I reach
AC0 or a bit less, and then I'm good for awhile yet.  But then, I'm not 
an early-game spellcaster either; the inconsistent supply of Pw for
non-wizards means that I cannot afford to rely on it, and if I can't afford 
to rely on it then I have to rely on something else.  That something
else might as well be a bunch of armor and a good weapon.

> Generally my breakpoint is DL8 due to the generation of polytraps. Until
> I've secured either MR or polycontrol, I'm reluctant to proceed.

This is an interesting dichotomy.  Polytraps aren't THAT big a deal.

IFF you become a "big" monster...
- You lose your armor, if any
- You lose your cloak, if any

In the early game, I definitely do not have my "rest-of-the-game" armor
on, and while sometimes I get lucky and find a cloak (or start with one), 
in other cases I'm just waiting for an elf to drop off some handy MC3.
'Til then I'll make do with whatever's uncursed and on the floor.

I would think that for a protection-racket character, polytraps would
become almost meaningless except from the cloak perspective -- since the
body armor is no longer required!

> In addition a good racketeer has buffed the pet so that by the time ants
> do appear, the pet is big and fast enough to tackle it. 

As someone else has pointed out, the ants will go around the pet unless
you can get them into a choke point.

> racketeer has gotten the divine AC by the time they encounter ants and
> has used that AC flexibility to either make themselves harder to hit,
> faster (not burdened), a more consistent spellcaster, or a combination
> of the three.

It's probably worth noting here that I never, _ever_ walk around
burdened.  Even if I can't outrun a monster, my actions are much more
powerful than the monster's, and losing those actions due to being
burdened is an absolute crime.  Against soldier ants, for example: I
cannot outrun them even if I'm not burdened, but if I'm not burdened I
can hammer down far more dust Elbereths and survive.

As far as AC flexibility, I'm not sure what flexibility is necessary;
"adding more whenever possible" is pretty much de rigeur up until about
AC -10.  That will last me 'til I'm ready to start the charge through
Gehennom, and by then I'll be in a position to upgrade a number of those
armor pieces.

Also mentioned above, I don't spellcast in the early game except as a wizard,
and even then I don't do very much of it; inability to control which spells
you get and ineffective Pw regeneration make spells an exclusively "out
of combat" thing... for which I can remove my armor as needed.

>>A more reliable and safer strategy is simply to go ahead and boost your
>>XL as fast as possible so that you get the extra HP, skill slots, and to-hit
>>bonus; this will work out much better for you in general, especially where the
>>hit bonus is concerned (since it will be easier to kill monsters who are
>>trying to eat your face.)
>
> That's the prevailing wisdom. And that wisdom is always backed by the
> fact that serial ascenders use this strategy, so it must be the right

Not necessarily "the only" right one.  But I would certainly say that
there's little question that it's "a" right one.

> one. But the counter argument is the fact that soldier ants are the
> number 1 killer of nethack characters on NAO. And that's a completely
> preventable encounter.

?? The counterargument seems to be a non sequitur.

Serial ascenders don't die to soldier ants in my experience.  I respect
the strength, to be sure, but they respect E, and that's all there is to it.
With the to-hit bonus from my XL, upgraded weapon skills, and HP I have
by the time I start seeing them, I'm not particularly worried; and I use
pettype:none, so I don't have a pet handy to clean them out for me while
I stand on my safe Elbereth.

The trick, of course, is not to wait 'til you're about to die to try
dust-engraving it.  When you see the ants coming (and you will), start
putting 'em down and/or look for a chokepoint to handle them in.

> To paraphrase Bill Clinton "It's the AC stupid!". All the items you list
> above are not helpful if you're getting whacked on at every turn.

To paraphrase in return, "It's the Elbereth."  Once I've got one down, I'm
antproof and have plenty of time and turns available to work out what,
if anything special, needs to happen.

> But in the early game managing XL can be a successful strategy because
> it does make the road easier while trying to attain the needed AC to
> contend with hazards down the road. And while there are clearly other
> roads to getting AC (I love dwarven mithril for example) they each have
> impacts on other aspects of game play (weight, spellcasting effect) that
> need to be accounted for. The Divine AC boost gives AC help in the early
> game without impacting those other factors.

I think my point (and where we differ) is about how important those
other factors are.  For example, I don't carry potions because I don't
need them in the early game and they're flammable/frigible; I can come
back for them later as necessary.  I carry scrolls for a bit longer, but 
they'll go too if things get heavy.  I don't spellcast except as a
wizard because I can't guarantee getting any spells worth casting.

Both of these mean that I can spend my strength obtaining AC in a very
straightforward fashion that _also_ walks toward the long-term goal of
ascension -- and without having to fear certain random circumstances
such as the gnome with a wand, the trapdoor that separates your pet from
you (or worse, vice versa), and a host of other pitfalls that strike 
during the protection run to jump the difficulty well out of
expectations.

> So it's a early game strategy for me. An alternative, not just the
> perfect path.

Which is really the part I'm coming from: the reliability
perspective.  The protection run _is_ an alternative (though
realistically, I go for protection as well in the early game, I just
don't bother making any effort to keep XL low to do so), ... but it has
enough pitfalls and things that can happen out of your control that I
consider it a less reliable alternative.  That's all.  :)

